-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions, resolved by a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins | A merge rule set cannot see the scientific relationships between fields, so it would sometimes combine related edits — a revised identification with the old cover estimate — without anyone noticing. A larger review queue is the accepted cost of never doing that. Last-write-wins discards a submitted edit. |
+| Current state of a contested visit | No single current state is returned | Return the most recently received revision | Arrival order is not evidence of authority; returning a provisional winner would let it reach an export unreviewed. |
+| Submission onto a contested visit | Accepted, extending the named revision | Reject until the contest is resolved | A tablet can be offline 48 hours and cannot wait on a coordinator; rejection would strand collected observations. |
+| Resolution outcome forms | Select a revision, or a coordinator-composed revision | Selection only | Selection alone drops the other worker's correction from the exported value whenever both competing edits are correct. |
+| Concurrent resolutions | Optimistic check against the named current-revision set | Last write wins; locking the visit during review | Two coordinators share the queue with no administrator to clear a stuck lock; a stale resolution must fail rather than apply to a contest its author never read. |
+| Divergence point in the contested response | Computed server-side | Client-side traversal of revision history | The server owns the revision graph; every client needing comparison would otherwise re-implement it. |
 
